ent that it's  happening at all well yeah and a  reminder that the offensive is not going  on just on the ground i guess um let's  move on to the other issue that's picked  up by a lot of the papers on their front  pages and that is the the sacking of  piano workers 800 of them kevin uh  you've picked out the metro's front page  first of all they talk about mutiny on  piano ferries by  the shocked workers who found out via  video call that they were to be made  redundant with immediate effect  yeah i mean it's a pretty remarkable  story i mean what a way to treat uh  human beings basically you know telling  them via zoom i mean it's the most  cowardly way possible no no warning no  no notice given they were just told  after a brief preamble that uh the  contracts would be terminated straight  away it was the last day of appointment  with piano ferries and understandably uh  these workers have responded with fury  uh they barricaded themselves uh into  the boats basically 800 of them refusing  to to to leave  the boats um and i mean even more  incredibly uh pinot ferries had a higher  security  uh personnel to go on board people who  were trained to use handcuffs to go on  and get the workers off the boats i mean  i talked about adding insult to injury i  mean it's quite an astonishing way to  treat anyone understandably there's been  political  fury the government have said uh you  know this is unacceptable it's no way to  to treat workers um  but right now it's difficult to see  actually what the government can  actually do about it and so there's a  bit of a standoff now between dubai who  own piano ferries and the unions  representing the workers um and it's  absolutely remarkable story and olivia  makes the front of the telegraph as well  shakes shut down shuts down piano in  battle with union i mean the company  themselves say the move was absolutely  necessary for its survival they say that  it's the only way they can keep their  business viable  um yes but i 